# Host-side configuration for the Windows sandbox worker.
# Install to /etc/default/honeypot-windows-sandbox (mode 0600 — it holds the
# guest password). Read by honeypot-windows-sandbox-worker.service.
#
# This file lives on the analysis host, not in the dashboard container. The
# dashboard never calls virsh, docker, or WinRM: it only writes a request into
# the spool below (IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md §7.1).

# ── Spool the dashboard shares with this worker ──────────────────────────
# Must match WINDOWS_SANDBOX_REQUEST_DIR / WINDOWS_SANDBOX_RESULTS_DIR in the
# dashboard's environment, or a submission is written where nothing reads it.
WINDOWS_SANDBOX_REQUEST_DIR=/windows-sandbox-requests
WINDOWS_SANDBOX_RESULTS_DIR=/windows-sandbox-results

# Where the captured samples themselves live. The request carries only a
# SHA-256; the worker resolves it here and refuses a request it cannot match.
WINDOWS_SANDBOX_SAMPLES_DIR=/var/lib/honeypot-sandbox/inbox/samples

# ── libvirt domain ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LIBVIRT_URI=qemu:///system
VM_DOMAIN=win11-sandbox
# Every detonation starts by resetting the domain to a fresh CoW clone of
# this golden image, so the guest is disposable and successive runs are
# comparable (Phase 2). Not a virsh snapshot -- see #358 for why.
GOLDEN_IMAGE=/var/dockge/sandbox/golden-images/win11-analysis.qcow2
VM_DISK=/var/dockge/sandbox/vms/win11-sandbox.qcow2

# ── Guest access (WinRM over the isolated sandbox bridge) ────────────────
# 10.10.10.0/24 has no route to the WAN; the host firewall drops forwarding.
VM_HOST=10.10.10.2
VM_USER=analyst
# Set a real value here and keep this file mode 0600. The credential only ever
# reaches a guest that is reverted after every run.
VM_PASS=changeme

# ── Detonation window ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Seconds to observe after execution. TimeoutStartSec in the service unit must
# stay comfortably above this multiplied by a plausible queue depth.
#
# #297: 300s (5 min) was never chosen for this reason -- it was an
# unexamined placeholder. windows_kimi/RESEARCH.md #4 item 7 and #1.3 both
# argue for 15-60 minutes: a sample doing nothing more sophisticated than
# Sleep(600000) before its real payload already defeats a 5-minute window,
# independent of any anti-detection work elsewhere in the image, and the
# living-persona daemon (#290) keeps GetLastInputInfo low for the whole
# window regardless of length. The cost is throughput -- fewer detonations
# per hour per available VM -- which is why this is a deliberate 30-minute
# default (the middle of the recommended range) rather than the top of it.
# Raise toward 3600 if long-sleep/date-gated logic bombs are a bigger
# concern than queue depth for your deployment; lower toward 900 if the
# reverse.
OBSERVATION_SECS=1800
